course: dictionary
lesson: 14859
title: Ratified: made official
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ratify is a formal word, often used about law and government. To ratify something means to make it official by voting for it or signing it to say yes. «The country ratified the treaty last year.» This means the country officially agreed to the treaty by voting or signing.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Treaty, agreement, law and parliament are all about official decisions, so ratified must be about approving something properly. Try your meaning in the sentence: «The country made the treaty official by signing it» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Ratified can also describe something that has already been approved this way. «Once the agreement was ratified, it became the law.» Here ratified tells you the agreement has been officially approved. A simple check is to ask: did someone vote or sign to say yes?
fact: Ratify means to make something official by voting or signing to approve it.
fact: «Ratified» is the past tense of ratify, and it can also describe something already approved this way.
fact: The other forms are ratify, ratifies and ratifying.
fact: «Parliament ratified the agreement» means parliament voted to approve it, so it became official.
fact: Countries ratify treaties such as the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child.
exercise: «The parliament ratified the new law.» What does ratified mean here? | *Made official by voting or signing to approve it | Argued about for hours | Painted in bright colours | Thrown away and forgotten
exercise: What is a good meaning for ratify in «Every country must ratify the treaty before it counts»? | Forget about | Ignore | Copy out by hand | *Approve officially by voting or signing
exercise: Which word is the past tense of ratify? | ratifying | ratifies | *ratified | ratify
test: «The school council ratified the new playground rules after a vote.» What does ratified mean? | Argued about | *Officially approved | Broken | Hidden
test: A government ____ the treaty after a vote in parliament. Which word fits the gap? | painted | cancelled | forgot | *ratified
exam: Which sentence uses ratified correctly? | *The class ratified its new rule by a vote | The children ratified their lunch box | The rain ratified the park | We ratified our wet socks
